CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows three rendered orbital shapes in a row on a white background, each captioned in bold beneath it, reading from left to right "An s orbital", "A p orbital", and "A d orbital". The leftmost is one translucent pink sphere, smooth and unbroken in every direction, with a small yellow-green bead at its center marking the nucleus. The middle shape stacks two lobes vertically, a rounded pink one above and a matching blue one below, meeting at a green nucleus bead; both are drawn as full, doorknob-like volumes rather than pointed teardrops, and they pinch away to nothing where they meet. The rightmost carries four lobes in a cloverleaf around a central green bead, their colors alternating around the ring: pink at the upper left and lower right, blue at the upper right and lower left. Color does the encoding, pink and blue standing for opposite algebraic signs of the wave function across a node, so the alternation shows the sign flipping with each crossing from one lobe to the next. The three together set out the orbital shapes and sign patterns that later account for bonding.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - s orbital: single translucent pink sphere, smooth and unbroken in all directions
  - s orbital nucleus: small opaque yellow-green bead at the sphere's exact center
  - p orbital: two rounded 'doorknob' lobes stacked vertically along the Y axis, pink lobe above, blue lobe below, each pinching to a point where they meet at the origin
  - p orbital nucleus: small green bead at the pinch point between the two lobes
  - p orbital nodal plane (horizontal XZ plane through the nucleus, implied by the pinch)
  - d orbital: four rounded lobes arranged as a cloverleaf in the vertical plane, pointing along the four diagonals; pink at upper-left and lower-right, blue at upper-right and lower-left; all four pinch to the origin
  - d orbital nucleus: small green bead at the center of the cloverleaf
  - d orbital's two perpendicular nodal planes crossing at the nucleus
  - bold caption text beneath each shape: 'An s orbital', 'A p orbital', 'A d orbital'
  - plain white background, no axes or box

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: s sphere radius 1.15 units, centered at origin, nucleus bead radius 0.09. p orbital: two lobes along +Y and -Y, each about 1.25 units long and 0.95 units wide at its fattest, fat end outward, tapering to a cusp at the origin (build as a revolved profile r(theta) = 1.25*|cos(theta)| about the Y axis, or two squashed spheres scaled 0.48 x 0.65 x 0.48 and offset to y = +/-0.72 with the inner ends pinched); pink lobe is the +Y one, blue lobe the -Y one; overall p height ~2.5 units, so it reads slightly taller than the s sphere is wide. d orbital: four identical lobes in the XY plane pointing along the diagonals at +135 deg (pink), +45 deg (blue), -135 deg (blue), -45 deg (pink) measured from +X, each lobe 1.15 units long, 0.85 wide, tapering to the origin; overall d width ~2.4 units, height ~2.4. Nucleus beads sit exactly at the origin of each shape and must render on top of the translucent lobes. When 'all three' is selected, place s at x = -3.4, p at x = 0, d at x = +3.4 with each shape's own origin on the y = 0 line; the single-orbital views center the selected shape at the origin. Bold captions on a plane at y = -2.1 directly under each shape, dark gray #1a1a1a. Colors: pink/rose lobes #dc9aa8 with a lighter #eebcc6 highlight, blue lobes #7fa6d4 with #a8c4e6 highlight, both translucent (opacity ~0.82) with soft matte shading and no hard specular; s-orbital nucleus yellow-green #bcc74a, p and d nuclei green #49a04f. Background pure white, no grid, no axis lines. Sign meaning: pink = positive wave-function amplitude, blue = negative; lobes sharing a color share a sign.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitalType",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "s, p and d orbitals differ in lobe count and node structure: zero nodes and one sphere, one node and two lobes, two nodes and four lobes",
    "label": "Orbital",
    "options": [
      "s",
      "p",
      "d",
      "all three"
    ],
    "default": "s"
  },
  {
    "id": "signHighlight",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "pink and blue are not decoration but opposite algebraic signs of the wave function; clicking one lobe lights every lobe of the same sign and shows the sign flipping across each node"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitView",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "each orbital is a genuine 3D volume — the p lobes are rotationally symmetric about their axis and the d cloverleaf lies in a single plane, facts invisible from the printed front view"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The s orbital",
    "narration": "A single pink sphere surrounds the yellow-green nucleus. The wave function has one sign everywhere and no nodes, so there is only one color.",
    "state": {
      "orbitalType": "s"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The p orbital and its node",
    "narration": "Two doorknob lobes meet at the nucleus. The pink lobe above and blue lobe below carry opposite signs; the pinch between them is a nodal plane where the wave function is zero.",
    "state": {
      "orbitalType": "p"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The d orbital: signs alternate",
    "narration": "Four lobes form a cloverleaf, pink at upper-left and lower-right, blue at upper-right and lower-left. Click any lobe to highlight its partner and see the sign flip on every crossing of a node.",
    "state": {
      "orbitalType": "d"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Compare all three",
    "narration": "Side by side, the progression is clear: 0 nodes and 1 lobe, 1 node and 2 lobes, 2 nodes and 4 lobes.",
    "state": {
      "orbitalType": "all three"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 15,
  "elevationDeg": 12,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Use MeshPhysicalMaterial with transmission/opacity ~0.8, roughness ~0.7, flatShading off, and DoubleSide so the translucent lobe interiors read like the airbrushed source renders; disable depthWrite on the lobes and sort them back-to-front so overlapping lobes blend softly instead of showing hard intersection seams. Nucleus beads use an opaque standard material with depthWrite on. Lobes can be built once as a single tapered-lobe BufferGeometry (revolved cos-profile) and instanced/rotated for the p and d cases; the s sphere is a high-segment SphereGeometry. Click picking: raycast against lobe meshes, then boost emissive and opacity on every lobe whose sign matches the picked one and dim the opposite-sign lobes to ~0.3 opacity; also fade in a translucent gray nodal plane (or two crossed planes for d) while a highlight is active. Toggle between orbital types with a short cross-fade / scale tween rather than an instant swap, and tween the selected shape to the center for the 'all three' -> single transitions. Lighting: one soft directional light from upper-front-left plus strong hemisphere ambient to match the flat, diffuse illumination of the original. Captions as camera-facing sprites or HTML overlays so they stay legible while orbiting.

Follow the interaction plan above.

The second image above is a screenshot of the PREVIOUS attempt's rendered output (not the source figure) — use it to see exactly what broke.

Here is the generated code to evaluate:

<!-- @FIGURE_UI_BEGIN -->
<div style="font-weight:700;font-size:12px;">Orbital</div>
<div id="orbRow" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px;">
  <button class="orbBtn" data-orb="s">s</button>
  <button class="orbBtn" data-orb="p">p</button>
  <button class="orbBtn" data-orb="d">d</button>
  <button class="orbBtn" data-orb="all">all three</button>
</div>
<div style="font-size:11px;color:#555;">The figure opens with all three side by side; pick one to enlarge it.</div>

<div style="font-weight:700;font-size:12px;margin-top:6px;">Sign highlight</div>
<div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px;">
  <button id="hlPos">+ (pink)</button>
  <button id="hlNeg">− (blue)</button>
  <button id="hlClear">clear</button>
</div>
<div style="font-size:11px;color:#555;">Pink = positive ψ, blue = negative ψ. Click any lobe in the figure to light every lobe of the same sign; the nodal plane(s) fade in.</div>

<div style="font-weight:700;font-size:12px;margin-top:6px;">Nucleus</div>
<label style="flex-direction:row;align-items:center;gap:6px;">
  <input type="checkbox" id="nucBig"> exaggerate bead size
</label>

<div style="font-weight:700;font-size:12px;margin-top:6px;">Guided tour</div>
<div style="display:flex;gap:4px;">
  <button id="prevStep">‹ Prev</button>
  <button id="nextStep">Next ›</button>
</div>
<div id="stepTitle" style="font-weight:700;font-size:12px;"></div>
<div id="stepText" style="font-size:11px;line-height:1.45;color:#333;"></div>

<div style="font-size:11px;color:#666;margin-top:6px;">Drag to orbit · scroll to zoom. Orbiting shows that the p lobes are rotationally symmetric about their axis while the d cloverleaf lies flat in one plane.</div>
<!-- @FIGURE_UI_END -->
// @FIGURE_CODE_BEGIN
/* ELEMENT SPEC
   s sphere        -> SphereGeometry(1.15,72,56)  MeshPhysicalMaterial pink #dc9aa8, opacity .80, depthWrite off, DoubleSide
   s nucleus       -> SphereGeometry(0.115,28,20) #bcc74a, depthTest off, renderOrder 999  + glow shell SphereGeometry(0.17) opacity .3
   p lobe (x2)     -> LatheGeometry(cusp profile t^1.3*(1-t)^0.5, L=1.25, W=0.95) pink +Y, blue -Y, cusp at origin
   p nucleus       -> SphereGeometry(0.115) #49a04f + glow shell
   p nodal plane   -> CircleGeometry(1.55) in XZ, gray translucent (fades in on highlight)
   d lobe (x4)     -> same lathe (L=1.15, W=0.85) rotated about Z: +135 pink, +45 blue, -135 blue, -45 pink
   d nucleus       -> SphereGeometry(0.115) #49a04f + glow shell
   d nodal planes  -> two PlaneGeometry(2.9,2.9) crossing at origin (XZ and YZ), gray translucent
   captions        -> HTML labels via addLabel, bold 15px, #1a1a1a, y = -1.98
   spacers         -> invisible tiny boxes reserving caption room for the camera auto-fit
   lighting        -> Hemisphere .55 + key Directional 1.05 upper-front-left + fill .3 + PointLight airbrush highlight
*/

const PINK = 0xdc9aa8, BLUE = 0x7fa6d4, NUC_S = 0xbcc74a, NUC_G = 0x49a04f;

/* ── lighting: soft matte, airbrush-like ──────────────────────────────── */
scene.add(new THREE.HemisphereLight(0xffffff, 0xece4e8, 0.55));
const keyL = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.05);
keyL.position.set(-4.0, 4.5, 6.0);
scene.add(keyL);
const fillL = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.30);
fillL.position.set(4.0, -1.5, 2.0);
scene.add(fillL);
const rimL = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.22);
rimL.position.set(1.0, -2.5, -5.0);
scene.add(rimL);
// soft hot-spot that gives the translucent surfaces a visible gradient
const spotL = new THREE.PointLight(0xffffff, 22, 18, 2);
spotL.position.set(-2.2, 2.4, 4.2);
scene.add(spotL);

/* ── lobe geometry: revolved cusp→fat→blunt profile ───────────────────── */
function makeLobeGeometry(L, W) {
  const f = t => Math.pow(t, 1.3) * Math.pow(1 - t, 0.5);
  let mx = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= 600; i++) mx = Math.max(mx, f(i / 600));
  const pts = [];
  const N = 72;
  for (let i = 0; i <= N; i++) {
    const t = i / N;
    pts.push(new THREE.Vector2((W / 2) * f(t) / mx, L * t));
  }
  const g = new THREE.LatheGeometry(pts, 80);
  g.computeVertexNormals();
  return g;
}

function lobeMaterial(color) {
  return new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({
    color: color,
    roughness: 0.68,
    metalness: 0.0,
    clearcoat: 0.12,
    clearcoatRoughness: 0.9,
    sheen: 0.45,
    sheenRoughness: 0.9,
    sheenColor: new THREE.Color(0xffffff),
    transparent: true,
    opacity: 0.80,
    depthWrite: false,
    side: THREE.DoubleSide,
    emissive: new THREE.Color(0x000000)
  });
}

/* nucleus bead: drawn last, on top of every translucent surface */
function makeNucleus(color) {
  const grp = new THREE.Group();
  const bead = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.115, 32, 24),
    new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
      color: color, roughness: 0.35, metalness: 0.05,
      emissive: new THREE.Color(color).multiplyScalar(0.45),
      transparent: true, opacity: 1,
      depthTest: false, depthWrite: false
    })
  );
  bead.renderOrder = 999;
  const glow = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.175, 24, 18),
    new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
      color: new THREE.Color(color).lerp(new THREE.Color(0xffffff), 0.45),
      transparent: true, opacity: 0.30,
      depthTest: false, depthWrite: false,
      side: THREE.BackSide
    })
  );
  glow.renderOrder = 998;
  grp.add(glow); grp.add(bead);
  grp.userData.parts = [bead, glow];
  grp.userData.base = [1.0, 0.30];
  return grp;
}

function makeSpacers(group) {
  const gm = new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.01, 0.01, 0.01);
